      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "We evaluated, using quantitative coronary angiography, the natural history of change that occurred in target lesions after successful sirolimus-eluting stent (SES) implantation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Percutaneous coronary intervention with drug-eluting stents (DES) has significantly reduced the rate of repeated target lesion revascularization. However, early studies have raised concerns regarding the \"late catch-up\" phenomenon of DES."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Between June 2004 and March 2007, consecutive 217 patients with 306 lesions without restenosis at early angiographic follow-up underwent late angiographic follow-up (early follow-up: 11.2 ± 2.1 months and late follow-up: 29.4 ± 5.2 months). Predictors of late catch-up were identified with univariate and multivariate regression analyses."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Although reference vessel diameter did not significantly change during follow-up [3.15 mm (interquartile range (IQR): 2.81-3.49 mm), 3.12 mm (IQR: 2.79-3.47 mm), and 3.08 mm (IQR: 2.76-3.46 mm) at postprocedure, and early and late angiographic follow-up, respectively; P = 0.2653], late loss (LL) significantly increased during follow-up [0.05 mm (IQR: 0.00-0.13 mm) and 0.08 mm (IQR: 0.01-0.19 mm) at early and late follow-up, respectively; P < 0.0001]. Univariate analysis showed previous intervention, adjunctive use of cutting balloon, lesion length, and progression of MLD, LL, %DS at early follow-up as predictors of late catch-up. Multivariate regression analysis identified %DS at early follow-up as a predictor of late catch-up (OR 1.076, CI 1.039-1.114, P < 0.0001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Significant and continuous progression of neointima after SES implantation was observed in the present study. Larger LL may be a sign of late catch-up phenomenon."
          }
        ]
      },
